  "P0030": {"description":"HO2S Heater Control Circuit (Bank 1 Sensor 1)","causes":["Heater circuit interrupted, e.g. broken or chafed wire near the exhaust","Heating element inside the sensor burnt out","Connector corroded or terminal pushed back","Heater supply fuse or relay faulty","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"],"repair":"Typical repair: medium difficulty, about 0.5–1.5 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on; Usually no noticeable drivability problem; Closed-loop control starts late after a cold start; Emissions test or readiness check failed. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P0031": {"description":"HO2S Heater Control Circuit Low (Bank 1 Sensor 1)","causes":["Heating element burnt out or high resistance (too little current)","Supply wire interrupted or fuse blown","Control wire to the engine control module interrupted","Corroded connector with high contact resistance","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"],"repair":"Typical repair: medium difficulty, about 0.5–1.5 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on; Closed-loop control starts late after a cold start; Further heater codes if a shared fuse has blown; Emissions test or readiness check failed. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P0032": {"description":"HO2S Heater Control Circuit High (Bank 1 Sensor 1)","causes":["Heater control wire shorted to battery voltage, e.g. chafed harness","Heating element shorted internally (resistance too low)","Water or corrosion in the connector causing leakage between pins","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"],"repair":"Typical repair: medium difficulty, about 0.5–2 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on; Closed-loop control starts late after a cold start; Fuse may blow repeatedly; Emissions test or readiness check failed. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P0033": {"description":"Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve A Control Circuit","causes":["Wire or connector interrupted, corroded or chafed","Solenoid coil or electric bypass valve faulty","Supply missing, e.g. fuse or main relay","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"],"repair":"Typical repair: medium difficulty, about 0.5–2 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on; Fluttering or hissing when lifting off the throttle if the valve stays closed; Weak response after gear changes; Too little boost if the valve stays open. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P0034": {"description":"Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve A Control Circuit Low","causes":["Control wire shorted to ground","Valve shorted internally","Supply missing, e.g. fuse or main relay","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"],"repair":"Typical repair: medium difficulty, about 0.5–2 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on; Fluttering or hissing when lifting off the throttle if the valve stays closed; Weak response after gear changes; Too little boost if the valve stays open. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P0035": {"description":"Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve A Control Circuit High","causes":["Control wire shorted to battery voltage","Loose contact or broken wire, e.g. harness vibrating from heat","Solenoid coil faulty","Output driver in the engine control module faulty"],"repair":"Typical repair: medium difficulty, about 0.5–2 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on; Fluttering or hissing when lifting off the throttle if the valve stays closed; Weak response after gear changes; Too little boost if the valve stays open. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P0036": {"description":"HO2S Heater Control Circuit (Bank 1 Sensor 2)","causes":["Heating element inside the sensor burnt out","Wire or connector damaged by heat or road spray","Heater fuse blown; a shared fuse can set several heater codes","Output driver or software of the engine control module"],"repair":"Typical repair: medium difficulty, about 0.3–1.5 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on; Usually no noticeable drivability problem; Catalyst monitor does not complete; Emissions test or readiness check failed. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P0037": {"description":"HO2S Heater Control Circuit Low (Bank 1 Sensor 2)","causes":["Control wire shorted to ground, or heating element burnt out (system-dependent)","Supply interrupted or fuse blown","Corroded connector with high contact resistance","Output driver or software of the engine control module"],"repair":"Typical repair: medium difficulty, about 0.3–1.5 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on; Usually no noticeable drivability problem; Catalyst monitor does not complete; Emissions test or readiness check failed. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P0038": {"description":"HO2S Heater Control Circuit High (Bank 1 Sensor 2)","causes":["Heater control wire shorted to battery voltage, e.g. chafed harness","Heating element shorted internally","Water in the connector causing leakage between pins","Output driver or software of the engine control module"],"repair":"Typical repair: medium difficulty, about 0.3–1.5 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on; Usually no noticeable drivability problem; Catalyst monitor does not complete; Emissions test or readiness check failed. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P0039": {"description":"Turbocharger/Supercharger Bypass Valve A Control Circuit Range/Performance","causes":["Valve sticking from carbon or oil mist, or diaphragm torn","Vacuum line or switching valve leaking (pneumatic design)","Position sensor in the valve faulty","Increased resistance in the wiring"],"repair":"Typical repair: medium difficulty, about 0.5–2 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on; Fluctuating or too little boost; Loss of power; Fluttering noise when lifting off the throttle. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P003A": {"description":"Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control A Position Exceeded Learning Limit","causes":["Electronic actuator or its control module faulty","Vane mechanism, sector gear or linkage worn or sticking","Replacement not learned in, or learning run aborted (e.g. low voltage)","Actuator lever broken","Position sensor or its wiring faulty"],"repair":"Typical repair: shop_only difficulty, about 1–3 h, usually a shop job. Symptoms: Check engine light on; Reduced power mode; Underboost or overboost codes possible; Sometimes no noticeable symptoms. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P003B": {"description":"Turbocharger/Supercharger Boost Control B Position Exceeded Learning Limit","causes":["Electronic actuator or its control module faulty","Vane mechanism, sector gear or linkage worn or sticking","Replacement not learned in, or learning run aborted (e.g. low voltage)","Actuator lever broken","Position sensor or its wiring faulty"],"repair":"Typical repair: shop_only difficulty, about 1–3 h, usually a shop job. Symptoms: Check engine light on; Reduced power mode; Underboost or overboost codes possible; Sometimes no noticeable symptoms. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P003C": {"description":"A Camshaft Profile Actuator A Control Performance/Stuck Off Bank 1","causes":["Oil level too low, wrong viscosity or sludged oil, so switching pressure is missing","Profile switching solenoid sticking or its screen clogged","Oil supply to the switching elements leaking or restricted, e.g. worn seals","Locking pin in tappet or rocker arm, or sliding cam piece, sticking","Consequence of a circuit fault of the same actuator"],"repair":"Typical repair: medium difficulty, about 0.5–10 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on; Lack of power at higher engine speed; Reduced power mode on some vehicles. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
