  "P0195": {"description":"Engine Oil Temperature Sensor A Circuit","causes":["Connector or harness damaged, corroded or oil-soaked","Sensor faulty internally (electronics fault on SENT sensors)","5 V reference or ground problem in the sensor circuit","Control module or software"],"repair":"Typical repair: easy difficulty, about 0.5–1.5 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on (depending on manufacturer); Oil temperature display missing or fixed; Restricted cam timing, slightly reduced power; Oil life calculation less accurate. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P0196": {"description":"Engine Oil Temperature Sensor A Range/Performance","causes":["Oil temperature sensor drifting or stuck (offset)","Comparison sensor (coolant, intake air) or its wiring wrong","Thermal management or coolant control valve, or thermostat faulty (oil genuinely too hot or cold)","Contact resistance in the connector","Control module software"],"repair":"Typical repair: medium difficulty, about 0.5–2.5 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on (usually after two drives); Implausible oil temperature reading; Possibly a cam timing code alongside; Often no noticeable symptoms. Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
  "P0197": {"description":"Engine Oil Temperature Sensor A Circuit Low","causes":["Signal wire chafed against ground or the engine block","Sensor shorted internally","Oil or water in the connector (leakage current)","Control module input faulty"],"repair":"Typical repair: easy difficulty, about 0.3–1.5 h, often DIY. Symptoms: Check engine light on (depending on manufacturer); Oil temperature display at maximum or oil temperature warning; Fan running constantly or power reduction possible; Restricted cam timing (Ford). Confirm with the factory service manual before replacing parts."},
